# The Rocci stack

This page is the composition: four layers, two application depths, and when
to stop. Exact forms live in [reference](/docs/reference/).

## Four layers

1. **Roc + Datastar.** Roc owns types, helpers, SQLite, and effects. Datastar
   is the browser transport: it sends intent and morphs HTML. It is not a
   client application framework and not a Rocci language feature.
2. **`.rocci` templates.** `@component`, `@css`, `@fixture`, markup, and
   directives. A PascalCase component lowers to a pure Roc `Html` function.
   Preview with `rocci view` / `rocci browse` without writing a server.
3. **Applications.** HTTP structure around those templates. Standalone and
   custom are two depths of this layer, not two products.
4. **Rocdown.** Optional Markdown-first documents and sites. Static pages stay
   Markdown; live pages reuse the application layer. This layer is more
   experimental than templates. Start at [Rocdown](/rocdown/).

You do not need Rocdown to ship an app. You do not need Applications if you
only preview components into an existing `main.roc`.

## Compile to Roc

A `.rocci` file is a Roc module. Rocci recognizes a closed set of `@`
declarations. Everything else is copied into generated Roc unchanged.
Roc type-checking starts after lowering. Tags become `Html.*` calls.
Component tags become ordinary functions.

```text
authored .rocci
    │ parse template regions, copy Roc regions
    ▼
generated .roc  ── Roc type-check and compile ── native host
    │
    └── source-map segments point diagnostics back at the template
```

Rocci does **not** type-check interpolations, params, or directive headers.
A template can parse and still fail in `roc`. The template crate does not
start HTTP or open a window; `rocci-cli` compiles the generated module and
serves it. The preview webview loads that same origin.

**Related:** [Generated Roc](/docs/reference/language/generated-roc/),
[compilation model](/docs/concepts/compilation-model/).

## Two application depths

| Depth | What you author | What `rocci run` does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Standalone | `@context`, `@init`, `@get:view`, optional fragment / command / live routes | Generate dispatch, `/assets`, `/health`, and authored live paths |
| Custom | `main.roc` plus `.rocci` modules | Compile sibling modules and start your Roc program |

Stay standalone until generated dispatch is in the way: extra routes, a
different platform, or a runtime the dispatcher does not express. Reuse
components; do not re-implement `Html`. Desktop bundling (`rocci bundle`)
wraps either depth in an ad-hoc macOS `.app`.

**Related:** [standalone versus custom](/docs/concepts/standalone-versus-custom/),
[Runtime](/docs/reference/runtime/).

## Deliberate limits

- Not JSX embedded throughout Roc.
- No runtime component registry and no magic `children` field.
- No markup inside arbitrary Roc expressions; use `@if` / `@for` / `@match`.
- No dynamic tag names or prop spreading.

Preview a component in [five minutes](/docs/start/five-minutes/), then
[templates](/docs/tutorials/first-component/) or a
[standalone app](/docs/tutorials/first-app/).
